{color:#006644}Hi Liis,{color}

{color:#006644}Could you please advise on a privacy related matter raised by our Bolt Food business colleagues in Portugal? {color}

{color:#006644}For context, in Portugal, one of the fleet courier companies that works for us (fleet A), wants to close its legal entity and transfer all the couriers it has currently employed to another company (fleet B). The owners of A and B might be different.  {color}

{color:#006644}Fleet A asked us to re-register  their couriers with the new fleet B in our app once we  terminate the contract with fleet A and sign a new one with fleet B. {color}

{color:#006644}Looking from the courier respective, some couriers (C) currently have an employment agreement with fleet A, and some couriers (D) currently have a service agreement with fleet A. Both are paid by fleet A at the moment and we do not know if fleet B will sign the same employment and/or service agreements on the same terms with all the couriers as of December.  {color}

{color:#006644}In any case, the legal department recommended to ask the individual couriers to sign up under a new fleet in Bolt’s courier registration form if they agree with it.{color}

{color:#006644}Our question here is whether, from a privacy point of view, it is okay for the business to communicate directly with the couriers considering that there is no relationship between us and them or whether there is any data privacy consideration we must be aware of. {color}

{color:#006644}Thank you very much\!{color}

{color:#ff5630}In principle, unless the contract with fleet clearly prohibits Bolt from reaching out to couriers directly, from data protection perspective this would be permissible and there are no immediate blockers to proceed. Both parties (Bolt and the fleet) are independent data controllers in relation to the courier data and this type of contact would be in line with Bolt Food privacy policy.{color}

