Where is the gps_timestamp used in courier endpoints:

For internal endpoints, the gps_timestamp is used in 

* {{"/internal/assignCourierToOrder"}} 
* {{/internal/rejectOrderByCourier"}} 
* {{"/internal/courier/acceptOrder"}} 
* {{"/internal/courier/rejectOrder"}} 
* {{"/internal/courier/pickUpPackages"}}
* {{“/internal/courier/arriveToClient"}}
* {{"/internal/courier/deliverPackage"}}

Where  it is inserted into order_courier_state_log table thorough a call to orderMoveState() or other flows.

For external endpoints, gps_timestamp is used in:

* {{"/deliveryCourier/acceptOrder"}} 
* {{"/deliveryCourier/rejectOrder"}} 
* {{"/deliveryCourier/pickUpPackages"}}
* {{“/deliveryCourier/arriveToClient"}}
* {{"/deliveryCourier/deliverPackage"}}

with the same usage as above.



However, the gps_timestamp (lat_lng_timestamp) is not used anywhere once stored in DB

* Unfortunately,  mysql-eventslog_filesize can not be set to zero[https://proxysql.com/Documentation/global-variables/mysql-variables/#mysql-eventslog_filesize|https://proxysql.com/Documentation/global-variables/mysql-variables/#mysql-eventslog_filesize|smart-link] 
* The Pythian article is referring to {{proxysql.log}} log file and not {{queries.log.0000000*}}. The numeric suffix is added by proxysql itself and not log rotate. What pattern to use in logrotate, {{/var/lib/proxysql/queries.log.*}}?
* Enable JSON ({{MySQL-eventslog_format=2}}), this has been done:
{noformat}mysql> SET mysql-eventslog_format=2;
Query OK, 1 row affected (0.00 sec)

mysql> LOAD MYSQL VARIABLES TO RUNTIME;
Query OK, 0 rows affected (0.00 sec)

